{
 "note": "St Mary's Anglican Church, corner of Queen St and Peel Tce (1844-45, the oldest stone church in WA; State Register). From the Landgate aerial (Oct 2026), Wikimedia Commons photos and the inHerit and Wikipedia records. Rubble limestone nave with a steep timber-shingle gable roof and coped gable parapets, the cream rendered plinth and stepped buttresses, the lower chancel and flat-roofed vestry at the east end, a small north porch, the bellcote and cross on the west gable, the separate timber bell tower with its pyramid roof south-west of the nave, about thirty graves among the trees to the south-east, and the church sign on a low wall by Queen St. Signs are plain lettering only. Metres: x left to right as seen from Queen St (north to south), y east from the west gable.",
